Execute queries against the board using a compact DSL.

Operations:
  get(ID)              Get a single element by ID
  activity(ID, ...)    Read bounded, cursor-paginated element activity
  list(filters...)     List elements with filters
  count(filters...)    Count elements matching filters
  summary()            Board overview (counts + active/blocked)
  plan(scope?)         Execution phases; scope=[ID,ID,...] plans the union of subtrees
  agents(stale=N?)     Agent dashboard
  models(agent?)       Available models for spawn
  project_config()     Full active project board runtime config (always local)
  project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER?)
                       Narrow effective child-spawn policy for one target
  provider_limits()    Machine-scoped provider rate-limit state (always local)
  provider_limits(identity=KEY|all_identities=true)
                       Report one indexed provider identity, or every one
  whoami()             Current remote URL and active user (always local)
  schema()             Introspect the full operations/fields/presets contract
  schema(operation=NAME|mutation=NAME)
                       Return only one typed operation or mutation signature

Field projection:
  { id status assignee }     Select specific fields
  { overview }               Use a base preset (minimal, default, overview, full, progress, planning)
  { observability-task }     Use project observability presets (project, epic, story, task)
  (omitted)                  Defaults to { id name status }

Batching:
  Separate multiple queries with semicolons; results preserve source order

Spawn policy projection:
  Full project_config reports the effective spawn-policy-v2/v3 contract,
  authority, normalized provider and role-effective policy, and exact config
  provenance. V2 additionally reports its frozen compatibility snapshot and a
  copy-ready v3 migration object; this is read-only evidence and never edits
  task-board.config.json. Spawn-preflight returns only the selected role policy
  and its actionable migration warning.

Output format:
  --format json       Standard JSON (default)
  --format compact    Compact tabular output (~40% fewer tokens)
                      plan() switches to the super-compact planning shape:
                      flat waves of IDs, one-line critical path, id->status map

Examples:
